---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RnaCanvas from '../components/RnaCanvas.astro';
import ReplicationCycle from '../components/ReplicationCycle.astro';
import { scienceImages } from '../data/images';

const toolkit = [
  { name: 'Molecular cloning', detail: 'Reporter viruses and constructs built in house' },
  { name: 'CRISPR/Cas9', detail: 'Knockout cell lines to test host factors' },
  { name: 'siRNA', detail: 'Fast, targeted knockdown screens' },
  { name: 'Next-generation sequencing', detail: 'Mapping viral and host RNA' },
  { name: 'Mass spectrometry', detail: 'Finding the proteins bound to viral RNA' },
  { name: 'Live and fixed imaging', detail: 'Following viral RNA through the cell' },
];
const micrographs = scienceImages.filter((i) => i.page === 'research');
---

<Base
  title="Research"
  description="The Courtney Group studies how influenza A virus RNA hijacks host proteins, and how that can lead to RNA therapeutics and equitable access to antivirals and vaccines."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <RnaCanvas />
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1>Viral RNA is never alone in the cell</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        From the moment influenza A virus enters a cell, its RNA is bound, modified, moved and read by host proteins. We want to
        know which of those partnerships the virus cannot live without.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="fundamental">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 · Fundamental molecular virology</p>
        <h2>Following influenza RNA through the replication cycle</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Influenza A virus has a genome of eight RNA segments. Every one of them has to be copied in the nucleus, exported,
          translated and delivered to the cell surface before a new virus can bud. Each of those steps needs the host.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ReplicationCycle />
      <div class="pillars">
        <article>
          <h3>RNA modification</h3>
          <p>
            Chemical marks on viral RNA, such as m6A, can change how stable it is, how well it is translated and whether the
            immune system notices it. David’s earlier work showed these marks enhance influenza gene expression.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Nuclear export</h3>
          <p>
            Influenza replicates in the nucleus, an unusual choice for an RNA virus. Its unspliced RNAs need host help to get
            out to the cytoplasm, where the translation machinery waits.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Trafficking and packaging</h3>
          <p>
            New genome segments travel to the cell membrane in vesicles before they are bundled into new virus particles. We
            have identified host proteins that ride along on that journey.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    micrographs.length > 0 && (
      <section class="section gallery-band">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="section-head">
            <p class="eyebrow">Under the microscope</p>
            <h2>What we are up against</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="micro-grid">
            {micrographs.map((img) => (
              <figure class="micro">
                <img src={img.src} alt={img.alt} loading="lazy" />
                <figcaption>
                  {img.caption} <span class="credit">{img.credit}</span>
                </figcaption>
              </figure>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    )
  }

  <section class="section" id="translational">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · Translation to advanced RNA therapeutics</p>
        <h2>Targets every influenza A virus shares</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Influenza changes quickly, which is why vaccines are updated every year and antiviral resistance keeps appearing. The
          host proteins the virus depends on change far more slowly.
        </p>
        <p>
          If an interaction between a host protein and viral RNA turns out to be essential to all influenza A viruses, it
          becomes a strong candidate for a new kind of antiviral. RNA medicines, from siRNAs to antisense oligonucleotides,
          are well suited to hitting those targets.
        </p>
        <p>
          David has worked on RNA therapeutics from the start: his PhD developed allele-specific siRNAs and CRISPR approaches
          for inherited eye disease. We are bringing that experience back to infectious disease.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="access">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 · Equitable access</p>
        <h2>Medicines that reach the people who need them</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The burden of respiratory viruses falls hardest on communities with the least access to new treatments and
          vaccines. A breakthrough that only reaches wealthy health systems is only half a breakthrough.
        </p>
        <p>
          We want the therapeutics that grow out of our work to be designed with affordability, stability and manufacturing in
          mind from the start, and we look for partners who share that aim.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section toolkit-band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How we work</p>
        <h2>A molecular toolkit</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="toolkit">
        {
          toolkit.map((t) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{t.name}</strong>
              <span>{t.detail}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="funding">
        Our core programme is funded by a European Research Council Starting Grant. We also study other RNA viruses, including
        HIV-1 and SARS-CoV-2, with collaborators.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .pillars {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 28px;
    margin-top: clamp(32px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .pillars article {
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    padding-top: 18px;
    border-top: 3px solid var(--saffron);
  }
  .pillars p {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.3fr);
    gap: clamp(24px, 5vw, 64px);
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  #translational {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--saffron) 8%, var(--bg));
  }
  #access {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--leaf) 8%, var(--bg));
  }
  .micro-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
  }
  .micro {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .micro img {
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    object-fit: cover;
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--masthead);
  }
  .micro figcaption {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .credit {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
  }
  .toolkit {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 300px), 1fr));
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .toolkit li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .toolkit span {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.96rem;
  }
  .funding {
    margin-top: 28px;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
</style>
